package Rendering2D

use Libraries.Containers.Array
use Libraries.Interface.Item2D
use Libraries.Game.Graphics.Camera
use Libraries.Compute.Matrix4

/*
RenderTree2D keeps track of where every item in a 2D scene is, so that a
Layer2D can draw only the items the camera can see. It is the 2D partner of
RenderTree3D. It answers these questions:

- Which items can the camera see? (CollectVisible)
- Which items overlap a rectangle, a circle or a point? (CollectInRectangle,
  CollectInCircle, CollectAtPoint), for example to find what the mouse is
  over.

This class only describes what a 2D render tree does. Most of its actions
are blank. Use one of the kinds of render tree, which can be swapped for one
another because they all have these actions:

- DynamicVolumeRenderTree2D, a tree of rectangles that fit around the items,
  like the one 2D physics uses. A good choice for most scenes, and for
  scenes whose items are bunched together unevenly, since the tree shapes
  itself around them and needs no region set ahead of time.
- QuadTree2D, which divides a region of the world into squares. A good
  choice for a large world whose items are spread out fairly evenly, such
  as a tile map, and where items move a lot, since moving an item rarely
  changes the tree.

An item's rectangle is the one its GetBoundingRectangle action gives, which
covers its corners after rotation and scaling.

Each answer is added to an Array the caller passes in. The caller empties the
array when it starts a new frame or search, with Empty(false), so the array
keeps its storage and no new one is made. The answers include hidden items;
the caller can leave them out with IsShowing or IsShowingWithParents.

A render tree does not watch items for changes. Whoever owns it, such as
Layer2D, calls Moved for each item whose position, rotation, scale or size
has changed, once per frame before drawing.

An item with no size, such as one only used to group other items, is not
added, and Add returns false. Its owner draws it without asking the tree.

The few actions here that are not blank are the same for every kind of
tree: CollectVisible, which turns the camera's view into a rectangle and
passes it to CollectInRectangle, CollectAtPoint, IsShowing and
IsShowingWithParents.

Attribute: Example

use Rendering2D.RenderTree2D
use Rendering2D.DynamicVolumeRenderTree2D
use Libraries.Interface.Item2D
use Libraries.Containers.Array

DynamicVolumeRenderTree2D volumeTree
RenderTree2D tree = volumeTree
Item2D item
item:SetSize(10, 10)
tree:Add(item)
Array<Item2D> found
tree:CollectAtPoint(5, 5, found)
output found:GetSize()
*/
class RenderTree2D
    /*
    This action adds an item.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to add.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item was added. False if it was already in
    the tree, or has no size.
    */
    action Add(Item2D item) returns boolean
        return false
    end

    /*
    This action removes an item.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to remove.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item was in the tree.
    */
    action Remove(Item2D item) returns boolean
        return false
    end

    /*
    This action removes every item.
    */
    action Empty
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if the item is in the tree.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to look for.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item is in the tree.
    */
    action Has(Item2D item) returns boolean
        return false
    end

    /*
    This action returns the leaf that holds an item, or undefined if the
    item is not in the tree. Finding it means a lookup in a hash table, so
    an owner that tells the tree about moves every frame should get each
    item's leaf once, after adding the item, keep it, and pass it to
    Moved(leaf) instead of the item. The leaf must not be used after the
    item is removed, and its setters must not be called from outside the
    tree.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to find.
    Attribute: Returns The item's leaf, or undefined if it is not in the tree.
    */
    action GetLeaf(Item2D item) returns RenderTreeNode2D
        return undefined
    end

    /*
    This action tells the tree an item's position, rotation, scale or size
    has changed. It looks up the item's leaf each time. Moved(leaf) does not.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item that moved.
    */
    action Moved(Item2D item)
    end

    /*
    This action does the same as Moved(item), for the leaf GetLeaf returned
    for that item, without looking it up. A leaf whose item has been
    removed is ignored.

    Attribute: Parameter leaf The leaf GetLeaf returned for the item.
    */
    action Moved(RenderTreeNode2D leaf)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items the camera can see to the results. The
    camera's view is found from its inverse combined matrix, so it should
    have been updated this frame. A turned camera's view is covered by the
    smallest rectangle around it, so a few extra items may be found.

    Attribute: Parameter camera The camera.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectVisible(Camera camera, Array<Item2D> results)
        Matrix4 inverse = camera:GetInverseCombinedMatrix()
        number minimumX = 0
        number minimumY = 0
        number maximumX = 0
        number maximumY = 0

        // Each corner of the screen, from -1 to 1 in x and y, is turned back
        // into a place in the world.
        integer corner = 0
        repeat while corner < 4
            number clipX = -1
            if corner mod 2 = 1
                clipX = 1
            end
            number clipY = -1
            if corner >= 2
                clipY = 1
            end

            number w = inverse:row3column0 * clipX + inverse:row3column1 * clipY + inverse:row3column3
            if w = 0
                w = 1
            end
            number x = (inverse:row0column0 * clipX + inverse:row0column1 * clipY + inverse:row0column3) / w
            number y = (inverse:row1column0 * clipX + inverse:row1column1 * clipY + inverse:row1column3) / w

            if corner = 0 or x < minimumX
                minimumX = x
            end
            if corner = 0 or y < minimumY
                minimumY = y
            end
            if corner = 0 or x > maximumX
                maximumX = x
            end
            if corner = 0 or y > maximumY
                maximumY = y
            end
            corner = corner + 1
        end
        CollectInRectangle(minimumX, minimumY, maximumX - minimumX, maximumY - minimumY, results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items overlapping a rectangle to the results.

    Attribute: Parameter x The x coordinate of the rectangle's left edge.
    Attribute: Parameter y The y coordinate of the rectangle's bottom edge.
    Attribute: Parameter width The rectangle's width.
    Attribute: Parameter height The rectangle's height.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectInRectangle(number x, number y, number width, number height, Array<Item2D> results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items whose rectangles hold a point to the results,
    such as the items under the mouse. Items are found by their rectangles,
    so a turned or round item may be found near its corners.

    Attribute: Parameter x The point's x coordinate.
    Attribute: Parameter y The point's y coordinate.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectAtPoint(number x, number y, Array<Item2D> results)
        CollectInRectangle(x, y, 0, 0, results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items whose rectangles overlap a circle to the
    results.

    Attribute: Parameter x The x coordinate of the circle's center.
    Attribute: Parameter y The y coordinate of the circle's center.
    Attribute: Parameter radius The circle's radius.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectInCircle(number x, number y, number radius, Array<Item2D> results)
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if the item is showing. It does not look at
    the item's parents, so an item whose parent is hidden still counts as
    showing.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item is showing.
    */
    action IsShowing(Item2D item) returns boolean
        return item:IsShowing()
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if the item and all of its parents are showing.
    A layer draws by walking down from each item, so a hidden parent hides
    its children. The tree holds items on their own, so this checks upward
    instead.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item and all of its parents are showing.
    */
    action IsShowingWithParents(Item2D item) returns boolean
        Item2D current = item
        repeat while current not= undefined
            if not current:IsShowing()
                return false
            end
            current = current:GetParent()
        end
        return true
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many items are in the tree.

    Attribute: Returns The number of items.
    */
    action GetSize returns integer
        return 0
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many levels the tree has below its top, or -1
    when it is empty. What a level is depends on the kind of tree.

    Attribute: Returns The number of levels.
    */
    action GetHeight returns integer
        return -1
    end

end
